==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|acordaunt}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|accord|ant}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈkɔɹd.ənt/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-accordant.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# In agreement; [[agreeing]].
#: {{syn|en|concordant|;|consonant|harmonious|corresponding|conformable|conforming}}
#: {{ant|en|discordant|;|dissonant}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Descent of Man|author=Charles Darwin|year=1871|passage=The breach of this law, even when the breach is known to be strictly '''accordant''' with true morality, has caused many a man more agony than a real crime.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Captivity An Oratorio|author=Oliver Goldsmith|passage=And now his voice '''accordant''' to the string.|year=1836}}
#[[harmonious]]
#* {{RQ:Longfellow Evangeline|passage=From the '''accordant''' strings of Michael's melodious fiddle}}

====Usage notes====
* Usually followed by ''with'' or ''to''.

